@startuml dyn-wake-on-first-job
!include include/palette.puml

title Dynamic: wake on the first job, from the release pipeline, step 0 of every app deployment and the app runbooks (ADR-IR33, ADR-IR34)

<style>
sequenceDiagram {
  participant {
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor #555555
  }
  .codefresh {
    BackgroundColor $C_CODEFRESH
    FontColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_CODEFRESH
  }
  .octopus {
    BackgroundColor $C_OCTOPUS
    FontColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_OCTOPUS
  }
  .azure {
    BackgroundColor $C_AZURE
    FontColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_AZURE
  }
  lifeLine {
    LineColor #999999
  }
  group {
    LineColor #777777
  }
}
</style>

hide stereotype
autonumber
actor "SRE On-call" as sre
participant "App runbook\ndb-restore or\nrun-acceptance-tests" as rb <<octopus>>
actor "App developer" as dev
participant "Codefresh\n<app>/release" as cf <<codefresh>>
participant "App deployment\n<app> to <env>" as appdep <<octopus>>
participant "platform-wake\nstep run-env-wake" as pw <<octopus>>
participant "Runbook env-wake\nplatform-infrastructure\nin infra-<tier>" as ew <<octopus>>
participant "aks-platform-<tier>" as aks <<azure>>
participant "apr-sleep-<tier>" as apr <<azure>>

note over aks, apr : Stopped by env-sleep; apr-sleep-<tier> enabled

== 1. Early wake: the release pipeline asks for nonprod and never waits ==
dev -> cf : merge to the app's default branch
cf -[$C_CODEFRESH]> ew : step wake_nonprod, after prepare and parallel with the gates: run env-wake in infra-nonprod through the Octopus REST API (key from context platform-octopus); the step never waits
cf -> cf : gates, images, signatures (the builds pool scales from zero)
ew -[$C_WAKE]> aks : az aks show: Stopped, so az aks start --no-wait (hosted-ubuntu, azure-platform-lifecycle-nonprod)
loop every 30 s until Running, up to Wake.TimeoutMinutes (20)
  ew -[$C_WAKE]> aks : az aks show
end
ew -[$C_WAKE]> apr : disable: alerts flow again
ew -> ew : step Wait for workers and gateway (Platform.OctopusApiKey): reads k8s-tdd and k8s-uat health, requests a check for an unhealthy pool without waiting; argocd-nonprod status for up to 2 minutes; Wake.CompletedAt

== 2. Step 0 of every app deployment: a keyless wake through platform-wake ==
cf -[$C_CODEFRESH]> appdep : octopus_release: release <VERSION>, channel Default; platform-standard deploys it to tdd (uat and prod by hand)
appdep -[$C_OCTOPUS]> pw : step 0 Wake environment: Deploy a Release of platform-wake to <env>, deployment condition Always; the deployment waits for its child
pw -> pw : maps tdd, uat to infra-nonprod and prod to infra-prod; reads PlatformWake.OctopusApiKey (set Platform Automation) and sends it only to an Octopus Cloud URL
pw -[$C_OCTOPUS]> ew : REST: run env-wake in infra-<tier> from refs/heads/main; queued behind a running env-wake or env-sleep of the tier (concurrency tag <environment-id>/cluster-power)
alt cluster Running (the early wake finished)
  ew -[$C_WAKE]> aks : az aks show: Running
  ew -[$C_WAKE]> apr : disable (idempotent)
else cluster Stopped (prod, or no early wake)
  ew -[$C_WAKE]> aks : az aks start; wait for Running, up to Wake.TimeoutMinutes
  ew -[$C_WAKE]> apr : disable
end
ew -> ew : step Wait for workers and gateway; Wake.CompletedAt
loop every 15 s, at most 60 minutes
  pw -[$C_OCTOPUS]> ew : REST: read the env-wake task
end
ew -[$C_OCTOPUS]-> pw : succeeded: in seconds when Running, about 5 to 10 minutes after a start; a failed wake fails the step
pw -[$C_OCTOPUS]-> appdep : child deployment succeeded
appdep -> appdep : steps 1 to 12: pins, verification, tests

== 3. App runbooks wait for a sleeping cluster and never wake it ==
sre -> rb : run db-restore in uat or prod, or run-acceptance-tests in tdd
rb -> rb : step Wait for the cluster to wake (hosted-ubuntu, no key): curl App.BaseUrl every 30 s, up to Wake.WaitMinutes (30)
alt the app answers
  rb -> rb : the runbook continues
else no answer in time
  rb -[$C_DANGER]-> sre : fails with guidance: deploy the latest platform-wake release to <env>, or run env-wake in infra-<tier>, then run again
end
@enduml
